sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch03-31> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Regeneration is a particular case of growth obeying the same laws; the regeneration curve (short latent period, rapid rise to a maximum velocity, then slow decline) only appears different because ordinary growth curves start at birth, and a complete growth curve from zero has the same essential features.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Regeneration is ordinary growth, with the same curve as growth from zero";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter III, “The Rate of Growth”; topic “Regeneration”. Scope: regenerating tissues and whole-organism growth"
}
